Experimental AME/Ztt support
===========================

The Ztt implementation in this directory is based on the draft AME/Ztt ISA
v0.6, specification commit 8d27acdcee7a189aad4e2907412e0c40a9d4b9e2.
The typed interface follows intrinsic v0.2.4 with the v0.2.5 zip/unzip value
interface and the separately agreed nominal Scalar design. This is a local,
experimental implementation, not a ratified ISA, stable API or psABI.

This source note applies to the Ztt definitions, builtins, header, RTL
patterns and corresponding AME tests. Repeated provenance comments are not
required at individual helpers or test cases. Keep comments that explain
state preservation, operand formation, allocation constraints or local
deviations; version provenance does not replace those explanations.

Instruction encodings remain provisional. The local writable CSR mapping
uses amestype=0x810, amefflags=0x811 and amexsat=0x812. These are not official
allocations and require matching Binutils and execution-platform support.
The architecture attribute alone does not identify this local mapping.

N is runtime-sized; UDS and M/ACC resources are selected by the compile-time
profile. Types and declarations do not imply that every hardware tuple is
supported. Typed values are function-local and require the documented
state and ownership restrictions.  Ordinary scalar/pointer calls can
clobber the M/ACC bank; live typed values are saved and restored, including
ACC descriptors, and typed operations prepare their required datatype.
Callees must return with compatible N/profile and enabled/owned state.
Before allocation, complete-group elementwise lowering checks simultaneous
M input and independent-result capacity.  A matching full source may hold
the result, and identical inputs share their group.  Infeasible assignments
are diagnosed before IRA without changing the requested datatype tuple.
Typed arguments/returns (including varargs) and a stable typed calling
convention are not provided.  Explicit ownership changes in typed functions
require a successful acquire-bit or ameown guard proven over the CFG.
Query-only observers retain the caller-owned entry contract.  A proved
query guard permits unknown entry ownership and keeps typed state setup
on the owned path; merely observing ameown does not imply such a guard.
After an exceptional edge, every typed function must prove ownership again
before typed accesses, even if it contains no ownership query or transition.
This can be guarded inside catch or after the join.  A handler that returns
or rethrows without further typed access needs no new ownership proof.
Release invalidates prior status proofs; late checking includes generated
register accesses and reloads.  N/profile must remain stable for the entire
function, including unowned intervals, and MS must already be enabled.
Unknown paths and raw selector mixing remain rejected.  This is not a
guarantee for exceptional exits or arbitrary ownership-changing inline asm.

The user interface is documented in gcc/doc/extend.texi. Compiler regression
tests live in gcc.target/riscv/ame and g++.target/riscv/ame under testsuite,
with independent ame.exp drivers. SAIL/Spike joint execution programs and
raw build/test archives are maintained outside the GCC source tree.

Definition catalogs
-------------------

The catalogs are grouped by their consumer and ordering contract, rather
than by the historical implementation batches:

  riscv-ztt-builtins.def          Raw instruction selector builtins.
  riscv-ztt-profile.def           Compile-time resource profiles.
  riscv-ztt-types.def             Ordered M/ACC type families.
  riscv-ztt-operations.def        Reusable operation lists.
  riscv-ztt-intrinsics.def        Ordered typed catalog and family includes.
  riscv-ztt-wide-intrinsics.def   Wide integer families.
  riscv-ztt-int-intrinsics.def    Small integer families and matmul.
  riscv-ztt-fp-intrinsics.def     Floating-point families and matmul.
  riscv-ztt-exponent-scalar.def   Exponent scalar interfaces.
  riscv-ztt-memory128.def         Storage-only 128-bit memory interfaces.
  riscv-ztt-zip-value.def         Paired-value zip/unzip interfaces.

The type and operation catalogs select sections with the corresponding
entry macros.  Consumers define only the sections they need, include the
catalog, and undefine those entry macros afterwards.  Define all type
entry macros for the ordered type enum and metadata table.  These files
intentionally have no include guard; the shared iterator helpers have one.

Catalog order determines builtin/type identities used by registration and
LTO.  Keep family and row order stable when reorganizing files.  In
particular, the exponent, memory128 and zip families retain separate late
registration positions.  Consolidation must compare complete expanded
records, not just names or counts.  It does not reduce the number of
interfaces or change the lazy-registration rules.  Update t-riscv whenever
the include graph changes so incremental builds track every catalog.

Registration reuses a GC-rooted leaf/nothrow input list.  Each declaration
receives its own attribute nodes; option pragmas and declaration-specific
attributes must not modify the shared inputs.

Ordinary function signatures use a temporary GC-rooted vector for each
used prototype.  Nominal Scalar signatures use a separate destination-block
cache, allocating the TC/arity vector only for a used destination type.
Three final slots share nominal dispatch placeholders by arity.  Operations
with the same destination reuse these signatures during registration;
the resolver still checks exact source and Scalar identities at each call.
Unused prototypes and destinations allocate no type vector.  Both caches
end at registration; later lazy declarations do not retain these caches.
Nominal signatures share immutable Scalar argument tails and reuse shorter
cached argument lists when adding M parameters.  The registration cache
roots those tails until their function types retain them.
Index, stride, offset and exponent signatures share three control tails
in the same temporary cache.  The size_t, int and long parameter types
remain distinct, including on RV32 where int and long have equal widths.
Load signatures share pointer argument lists by exact C pointer type,
separately for plain and strided loads.  Temporary GC-rooted maps retain
these lists without merging result types or pointer element identities.
Ordinary M-only binary and ternary signatures similarly prepend one
parameter to the cached unary or binary argument list.
Exponent accumulation reuses the ordinary exponent parameter tail.
Column ACC-to-M transfers reuse the row transfer's ACC parameter list,
while retaining their distinct result shape.
Cache misses build those prefixes in arity order without recursive lookup.
Store, concat and M-to-ACC signatures also share the cached M argument tails,
retaining their pointer, stride, shape and return types.
Matmul signatures reuse these M argument tails and prepend the old ACC;
different input layouts share the right-hand M tail only.
Lazy internal matrix, Scalar, unary, exponent and matmul declarations share
GC-rooted unsigned metadata tails.  Each signature prepends its value
arguments without modifying the shared suffix.
Explicit Scalar registration reuses the current operation/type name prefixes
in a local two-spelling cache while iterating over Scalar source types.
Exponent interface names use the same bounded constructor and existing M type
suffixes, without reformatting the datatype and shape for each declaration.
Unavailable destinations skip their complete TC group in the compact Scalar
catalog.  Available entries retain their original order and signature checks;
stored non-Scalar entries, including overloaded stores, keep individual gates.
Sequential registration decodes compact Scalar metadata at group boundaries
and advances the prototype ordinal within each contiguous interval.  The
narrow/wide TC boundary and skipped groups restart decoding.

Scalar resolution indexes exact main-variant type identities on first use.
The GC/PCH-visible reverse index grows only through the catalog prefix
needed by a lookup.  Repeated calls avoid rescanning that prefix; unrelated
record types remain invalid, and non-record arguments allocate no index.
Type UIDs provide stable hashes across PCH restore; equality remains exact.
Scalar arithmetic resolution consumes a read-only argument view.  Nominal
calls unwrap their payload into at most three stack-local argument slots,
without allocating a temporary GC vector or modifying the caller's list.

Dense matrix, scalar, broadcast, unary, math and exponent declaration tables
use GC/PCH-visible pages of 256 declarations, allocated on first insertion
into each page.  Empty lookups allocate nothing.  Code indexing uses the
page number and slot; unused pages allocate no declaration storage.
The public catalog and nominal constructor/operation tables use the same
layout.  Unregistered tables expose only empty directory roots to GC,
rather than scanning their full code ranges.  Availability
checks still precede nominal cache lookup.
LTO validates public codes on demand with the registration-time profile,
using the same signature eligibility rules as header registration.  Streamed
declarations need no full public-catalog scan or placeholder table.
Header registration passes each decoded catalog entry to the signature
check, avoiding a second metadata lookup for the same code.  The catalog
loop binds its profile once instead of repeating the option-name lookup.

Public Scalar declaration blocks omit leading TC slots owned by older
declaration domains.  The storage offset does not change public code radices,
registration order or nominal type identity.

M-to-ACC preparation reuses each complete source packet's known Md.
Unknown packets retain their payload across datatype setup using the existing
private slot; destination Ad setup and ACC transfers remain unchanged.

Datatype name fragments are cached by descriptor and alias kind.  The
profile-independent cache contains text only; declarations, type nodes
and availability remain under the existing registration rules.
Explicit-TC and overloaded Scalar names share an operation-name builder that
reuses the existing M type-name datatype/shape fragment.
ACC shapes and aliases without a catalog spelling retain segmented assembly.
No extra complete-name or shape-name cache is allocated.

Type register counts are cached for the active profile and cleared when
it changes.  The cache contains counts only, not trees or modes.

Nominal Scalar lookups check availability before returning a cached
declaration, without rebuilding its name or function type.
Internal nominal broadcast declarations use the same paged table; public
constructors and overload declarations retain their existing storage.
Overload registration collects available M destinations once, in type-code
order, and reuses that list for each operation.  Operation-specific gates
and subsequent lazy lookups retain their availability checks.

Scalar registration keeps one temporary entry per destination type/shape,
with a shared decoded TC array.  Operations expand each group's TC range in
code order; floating restrictions and legacy integer entries are unchanged.
Broadcast registration shares these decoded indices.  Ordinary declaration
lookups still validate the code and active profile through their decoders.
Function-type lookup uses the Scalar ordinal already encoded in those
builtin codes, without converting its type descriptor back to an ordinal.

The exponent Scalar catalog uses ZTT_EXPONENT_FAMILY to store family
descriptors and a shared type axis.  Expansion remains operation-major,
type-minor to preserve builtin codes and LTO identities.  Names are built
after profile/type checks.

The static builtin catalog stores prototype, expansion and type ordinals
as unsigned shorts, with compile-time range checks.  Lookup restores the
original enum types and builtin identities.  Code-limit scans use the stored
records directly; exponent families do not belong to either anchor domain.
Integer Scalar and broadcast entries store shared operation names.  Their
canonical names use the existing destination/source type builders and local
prefix cache after profile checks; public names and code order are unchanged.
Eligible default Scalar names use the same builder and cache instead of
rescanning complete canonical spellings for rounding suffixes.
The contiguous wide Scalar interval stores one descriptor per operation and
decodes destination/TC ordinals using the existing type axis.  Narrow
destinations in this interval have wide TC entries only.  Narrow Scalar
records use four historical intervals and a family-order map over the same
operation descriptors and type axis.  Compact stored indices map back to
public codes without changing either narrow or wide interval boundaries.
Lookup partitions codes at the wide interval: later entries bypass the
narrow interval scan and subtract its complete prefix directly.
Store lookup scans only stored metadata, since the compact Scalar families
contain no stores.  Matches map back to the original public codes; the
cache retains the first matching code and the missing-entry sentinel.
Column extract/transfer and matmul redirection also scan stored records.
Their cache maps only eligible records back to the first public code;
compact Scalar families do not enter these redirect domains.
Extended integer unary anchors use the same stored-record traversal.
Compact Scalar and exponent families cannot supply unary anchors; the
numeric cache retains original first-match codes and missing results.
Wide anchor lookup still includes Scalar families, but checks only the first
TC of each compact destination group.  Later TC variants cannot match the
canonical prototype; group boundaries and public anchor codes stay intact.
Wide matmul anchors scan stored records only.  Compact Scalar and exponent
families contain no matrix-multiply entries; cached anchors retain public codes.

Structural folding
------------------

Unary, scalar, elementwise and matmul operations can read a single-use concat's
halves when each contains complete hardware operands in the same local window.
Packet offsets stay within each half and preserve the original operation order.
Elementwise inputs are checked independently with their own packet widths.
Matmul inputs retain their row/column shapes, paired windows and ACC fold order.
Their packet widths must match to avoid mixed-width spill pressure.
The concat access remains visible to ownership checking; the instruction
order and destination formation are unchanged.  A single-use same-mode
carrier view can be traversed when its original type matches exactly.
Scalar conversion, exponent controls and ternary old destinations retain
their original expansion and evaluation order.
Ternary and mixed-width consumers keep the concat allocation when both halves
are single-use.
Same-mode carrier views replaced by TER do not block this substitution.
Other intervening M definitions remain boundaries.
Typed stores can use the same complete-half sources without changing their
addresses, strides, packet order or alignment-staging paths.
M-to-ACC can retain separate complete-half sources for a single local ACC
consumer with room left in the M bank.  The result stays whole until allocation;
post-reload splitting uses two existing transfers at whole packet boundaries.

The GIMPLE hook can replace extraction from a single-use concatenation
with the corresponding complete source value.  Exact types and shape
transitions must match.  The search stays within one basic block and
crosses only a bounded number of memory-free SSA copies or debug statements.
Calls, asm and state operations remain boundaries.  The concatenation
call is retained for ownership checking.  A shared pair may also fold when
all uses are complete, same-type extractions in the same 16-statement
window.  Full-value uses and intervening calls, asm or memory accesses keep
their existing lowering.

Up to four same-type M-to-M copies may occur between concatenation and
extraction.  Each intermediate value must have one use and consist of a
complete register group, except for the final shared pair checked above.
Copies remain as ownership witnesses.

The local structural window can include ordinary integer or pointer SSA
calculations with no memory effects or possible traps.  All operands must
have integer or pointer types; floating comparisons are not an exception.
These calculations stay in place, and the 16-statement bound is unchanged.
The window also permits builtin amenlen and ameudsz reads, preserving each
read and its order.  Other CSR observations and ordinary calls remain
boundaries; builtin identity, not the function name, determines eligibility.
Rebuild and inverse-permutation inputs may also have later complete typed
stores in the same local window.  Those stores retain the intermediate
values; other extra users or an intervening state boundary prevent folding.
For inverse permutations, a retained intermediate must be stored after the
result, or the parent must already have a later store.  Otherwise the inverse
can consume the intermediate in place, avoiding an extra parent copy.

A concatenation of complementary halves from the same parent can also
reuse that parent.  Apart from the later stores, each half has one use;
the parent's only value uses must be the two extractions.  The same local boundaries apply;
extraction calls remain as ownership witnesses.  Reversed indices and extra
live values other than the permitted stores keep their existing lowering.

Each half may pass through up to four same-type M-to-M copies.  Every copy
result and extracted half must have one use, except for later stores of the
final half.  All calls and witnesses must fit in the same 16-statement
local window.  Longer chains and
fractional-register halves do not match.

Same-axis zip/unzip pairs can reuse their input in either order.  They
require the same complete 1x2 type, one value use of the input and
intermediate (apart from its later stores), and the same local boundaries.
The inner call remains for ownership checking.  Different axes, repeated permutations and packed
types do not match.

Up to four same-type M-to-M copies may occur between the inverse operations.
Every intermediate value must have one use, except for later stores of the
final copy; all copies join the same bounded window.  Inner layout and copy
calls keep their operands for ownership checking.  Longer chains and other
extra live values do not match.

Resultless typed extract and zip calls can remain as ownership witnesses
without counting as parent value uses.  Every use and witness must occur
in the same bounded local window.  The calls are retained; numeric, raw,
nonlocal and genuinely live uses do not gain this exception.

A resultless same-type M copy can also be a witness.  An extract or zip
result may have one use by such a copy; both calls join the bounded window
and retain their arguments.  The check does not follow further copies.

Reconstruction and inverse permutations also accept parent uses by complete
typed stores in the next 16 statements of the same block.  These uses keep
the original value live across the fold.  The forward window crosses only
typed stores and local scalar assignments.  Earlier stores, nonlocal uses,
live intermediates and later ownership-only witnesses do not qualify.

Column/row gathers with the matching integer column/row index constructor
can reuse their data input.  The constructor remains for ownership and
index-range checks.  It must be in the preceding 16-statement local window;
calls, memory accesses, asm and state changes prevent the fold.  The data
and result must have the same complete basic-square type.  Floating data
is selected bitwise, without conversion or rounding.  Opposite axes and
externally supplied indices keep their existing lowering.

The index may pass through up to four same-type M copies in that window.
Integer conversions may also occupy these four steps when the destination
covers the source's nonnegative range, comparing width minus the sign bit.
This includes widening, equal-width RM/SAT changes and signed-to-unsigned
conversions.  Both types must describe an unpacked basic square.  Constructors,
conversions and copies remain as GIMPLE ownership witnesses.  Narrowing,
equal-width unsigned-to-signed and floating conversions do not qualify.  Inner
inputs require a single value use; other uses of the final index remain.

Integer scatter-max with identical old/data SSA inputs and exact datatypes
also reuses that value when the index is a matching row/column ID.  It uses
the same bounded index search and retains constructor checks.  Scatter-add,
floating scatter-max, distinct old values and loaded indices are unchanged.
Old/data operands may each pass through four complete same-type copies from
a common origin.  Shared copies remain live, and the old operand replaces
the result without extending the common ancestor's lifetime.  These copies
participate in the same local-window check as the index constructor.
One same-mode carrier view per operand is unwrapped using its exact nominal
type, as for the index; the views remain part of that local proof.

Integer scatter-add with a matching-axis ID reuses its other operand when
the old or data input is a zero constructor of the exact datatype.  Up to
four complete copies and one carrier view may follow that constructor.
Its GIMPLE ownership witnesses remain; ordinary RTL DCE may remove the
unused zero value.  Loaded zeros, floating addition and arbitrary indices
do not qualify.

Same-datatype integer addition, OR, XOR and unsigned max reuse the other
complete value when either operand is an explicit zero.  MSUB computes B-A,
so its first operand must be zero.  AND-NOT computes A & ~B and accepts a zero
second operand.  The bounded zero proof applies to grouped M values.  Mixed
datatypes, floating arithmetic and intervening state boundaries do not
qualify, and the zero constructor remains an ownership witness.

Integer min/max/and/or of the same complete value reuse the first operand.
The inputs must have identical datatypes and shapes, and may follow up to
four complete copies each.  Exact carrier views and the common typed
producer must be in the local structural window.  The producer and copies
remain visible to ownership checking; floating values and state boundaries
do not qualify.

The same integer operations reuse an inner result when the other outer
input repeats either inner input.  Both operators and datatypes must match.
Up to four complete copies may occur on each path, within the same local
window.  The inner operation, its loads and the copies remain; unrelated
inputs and state boundaries keep the outer operation.

Integer AND/OR and min/max pairs also apply absorption: an
outer operation combining the inner result with an inner input reuses
that input.  The inner result and its intervening copies must have single
uses, so the rewrite does not keep both that result and the original input
live.  The other inner input must feed a subsequent complete local store,
retaining its producers and loads.  The same type, copy-depth and local-window
checks apply.  Ownership witnesses remain; floating pairs do not qualify.

For an inner A & ~B, outer AND with A reuses the inner result; outer OR
with A reuses A under the absorption conditions above.  Only the first
inner operand matches: AND-NOT is not commutative.

Integer AND and unsigned min with an explicit zero reuse that complete
zero value.  The other input must feed a complete local store, preserving
its producers and loads.  Exact datatypes and the bounded local zero proof
are required; signed min and floating operations do not qualify.
AND-NOT uses the same absorption proof only for its first input: 0 & ~A = 0.
Integer multiply and negate-multiply also reuse an explicit zero operand.
An exact-type carrier view of the other input is included in the local
proof before checking its complete store.  Floating and mixed datatypes
retain their arithmetic.

Integer multiplication by an exact same-datatype broadcast of one reuses
the other complete value.  The bounded local proof retains the broadcast
and copy witnesses; floating, mixed-type and negate-multiply calls remain.
The same proof handles AND with a full all-one mask and unsigned min with
the maximum value.  All-one broadcasts must fit XLEN; a wider Scalar is
zero-extended and cannot supply the high mask bits.

Repeated same-type abs, prefix-max and min/max reductions reuse the first
result when the complete type and operation match.  The bounded local copy
proof retains the first operation and its status effects, including floating
NaN canonicalization and NV.  Floating carrier views preserve exact datatype
and rounding-mode identity.  Different axes, datatype conversions and state
boundaries do not match.
Integer extrema also reuse values replicated along their axis by row/column
broadcasts or index constructors, retaining the producers and range checks.
Floating layout producers still require their numerical NaN handling.
Same-axis min/max reductions and prefix-max also reuse a preceding sum or
extremum reduction.  Its replicated result is retained independently in
each logical square; the first reduction and its status effects remain.
Extrema also reuse uniform integer broadcasts.  Typed zero constructors
are fixed points of abs and extrema for integer and floating types; dynamic
floating broadcasts retain their NaN processing.  Constructors and argument
side effects remain in the local proof.

Same-input integer XOR, AND-NOT and subtraction on complete multi-part tuples
use typed zero construction.  A bounded local proof requires an independently
stored input and preserves its producer.  Single-part and packed values retain
the binary operation.
One exact-type carrier view per operand is unwrapped before checking that
store.  The view remains in the bounded proof; arithmetic conversion and
mixed datatypes do not qualify.

An integer XOR pair cancels a repeated input, returning the other inner
input.  The inner result and intervening copies must have single uses,
and the repeated input must feed a complete local store so that its loads
remain.  Exact datatypes and the same bounded local proof are required.

Consecutive row/column broadcasts with the same axis and complete
basic-square type reuse the first result within that local window when their
controls match, their constant low 32 bits match, or the second control has
constant zero low 32 bits.  Index zero is valid for every positive N.
The first broadcast retains its range and ownership checks.  Floating
broadcasts copy element bits without conversion or rounding, preserving
NaN payloads and signed zeros.  Other differing controls and intervening
state or memory operations do not qualify.

Index-zero row/column broadcasts also reuse same-type zero and nominal
scalar-broadcast results within that window.  The original constructor and
scalar conversion remain; nonzero and dynamic indices retain their checks.

Gathers from these uniform values reuse the input when an independent local
store keeps the complete index value live.  Both operands must be non-packed
basic Squares.  Gather indices wrap by masking, so arbitrary integer index
bits are valid.  Original constructors, conversions and user loads remain.
The same proof covers row broadcast/gather of column indices and column
broadcast/gather of row indices: each selected row or column is identical.
Index constructors retain their runtime representability checks, broadcasts
still require zero low control bits, and gathers retain the index store.
Same-axis gathers also reuse row/column broadcast results.  The original
broadcast keeps its dynamic control, argument side effects and range check;
integer and floating element bits pass through unchanged.
An adjacent pair of same-type orthogonal broadcasts is uniform on both
axes.  Gathers and index-zero broadcasts reuse that result when both
producers satisfy the same local proof.  Both original range checks remain.
Column reductions are invariant under row selection, and row reductions under
column selection.  Gathers and index-zero broadcasts reuse their complete
results while retaining the reduction, conversions and status effects.
This proof can cross one elementwise conversion, abs or floating unary
operation between unpacked basic Squares.  Rounding, exp/log, trigonometric,
reciprocal and square-root operations retain their computations and status
effects; calls and memory effects still bound the local proof.
The same bound covers one same-type elementwise binary operation whose two
inputs are invariant along that axis.  Generic carrier calls revalidate their
explicit datatype metadata; equal physical modes alone do not establish this
proof.  The operation remains, including rounding, saturation and floating
flags; neither input's live range is extended.
Difference, half-difference, mean, comparison and conditional selection use
the same proof.  Predicates, selected data and any old destination must all
have the result type and invariant axis; the original computation remains.
Matrix shifts and OR-NOT use the same proof.  Both data and shift-amount
matrices must be invariant on the selected axis and have the result type;
the shift operation and its full-width count interpretation remain unchanged.
The same proof covers three-input elementwise multiply/add/subtract operations
when all inputs, including the old destination, have that invariant layout.
The arithmetic remains unchanged; only a following redundant layout operation
is removed, within the existing local statement and witness bounds.
One data-Scalar operation also preserves the source axis when its M input and
result have the same logical type.  The validated scalar may have a different
TC/RM; its conversion and the original operation remain.  Old-destination
multiply/add/subtract forms require both M inputs to have that invariant
layout and the result type.  Intervening memory accesses still bound the proof.
Signed-long exponent controls preserve the same axis in mldexp and mldexpacc.
The latter requires both the old value and M source to have the invariant
layout; exponent evaluation, arithmetic and status effects remain unchanged.
Matrix ldexp/rdexp and their accumulate forms also preserve an invariant
axis when data, exponent, result and any old destination share one integer
type.  Every M input must satisfy the same single-layer layout proof.
Floating carrier calls are checked against their exact destination metadata.
Zero constructors record their consumed descriptor GPR so later loads can
reuse it without rebuilding the same constant; the clearing setter remains.
Integer scatter-add with an exact zero contribution likewise reuses its old
destination for arbitrary masked indices kept live by a complete local store.
Both operands remain non-packed basic Squares; floating additions and a zero
old destination alone do not satisfy this rule.
Integer scatter-max also reuses its old destination for an unsigned zero
contribution, or when old/data are copies of the same uniform constructor.
The independent index store and bounded local proof remain required.  An
arbitrary nonuniform self-input does not imply an identity under scatter.

The bounded copy search also recognizes zero-offset row/column shifts of
complete basic Squares, using their existing full-value copy semantics.
Such shifts may serve as terminal ownership witnesses.  Repeated broadcasts
use this search too, retaining the final copy as the replacement value.
Nonzero/dynamic offsets and longer or shared-inner chains remain boundaries.
Separately, a local same-type zero constructor remains zero under any signed
row/column offset, including floating positive zero.  Complete non-packed
basic Squares reuse the original zero value and retain scalar side effects.
Calls, asm and observable memory operations still bound the local proof.

An optimized runtime-profile integer mconv with identical source and
destination type indices expands to a full-mode M copy.  Width, signedness,
RM, SAT and shape must all match.  The move retains the early ownership
access; floating conversions and other unary operations are unchanged.
The bounded structural-copy search recognizes these integer conversions
through their extended builtin codes.  Matched conversions remain as
ownership witnesses, including unused parent and terminal conversions.
Integer and floating matrix identity gathers use their datatype metadata, not
the physical carrier's type.  The index may have one same-mode carrier
view before the bounded copy chain; its constructor remains in place.
When a separate destination would exceed the profile's M register count,
wide gathers tie it to a complete same-datatype data source. Early-clobber
protects a distinct index; source preparation supplies the shared Md.
When both sources have that full type, either may supply the destination.
Optimized runtime gathers offer independent and matching destinations for
a dying complete data input distinct from the index.  Bank-limited groups
retain their tied early-clobber alternative.  In leaf functions, a dying
index with the same complete type as live data permits either source to
supply the destination without forcing a tie.  A dying identical data/index
value also permits complete destination overlap when both descriptors and
packet steps match.  Live shared inputs retain their allocation choices.
Fixed-exponent accumulation can also reuse a dying input shared with the
old destination on optimized runtime profiles.  The complete mode and Md
must match; distinct inputs are not unified.
Data-scalar old-destination operations use this same complete-value rule.
Their TC setup, ingress conversion flags and arithmetic remain intact.
Matrix ternary operations can similarly reuse a dying old/source value
when its complete mode and datatype match the destination.  If only one
input matches, it is tied to the old destination while early-clobber
keeps the other input independent.  This includes floating data with an
integer matrix exponent.  Bank-limited matrix ternaries share identical
nonvolatile arguments during expansion and allow complete old/source reuse
even at O0 or with a live original.  Distinct core inputs must fit the M
bank; impossible assignments are diagnosed before allocation.  Other live
old values retain independent allocation.
For groups wider than four registers, three identical complete inputs with
a dying old destination use one matching reload operand outside bank limits.
The shared-input proof follows up to four exact-type SSA copies, including
explicit mcopy, through one same-mode carrier view. Copy calls stay in
place for ownership checking. Loads, volatile accesses, conversions and
partial-group operations do not establish this identity.
Wide binary and scalar ternary inputs use the same complete-copy proof
when distinct inputs would exceed the bank.  Whole-bank scalar
accumulation ties its shared source and old destination as one reload
operand, including when the original value remains live.
Optimized runtime fixed-exponent accumulation also uses this shared
operand outside the bank limit for groups wider than four registers.
The same complete-copy proof applies; allocation preserves a live original.
Scalar datatype setup and TC-to-TB conversion remain at the scalar core.
Bank-limited gather likewise shares identical complete data and index
values, while keeping the gather operation and its index semantics.
Scatter can share the same complete data/index pair when separate inputs
would exceed the bank.  Its tied old destination stays independent of both
sources; capacity checks include that group before register allocation.
Mixed elementwise operations allow a complete compatible source to match
the destination or occupy a separate group.  Early-clobber still excludes
the incompatible input, avoiding a forced copy of a live compatible source.
Optimized runtime scalar operations also allow complete-type source reuse
for small M groups.  Matrix forms retain their existing allocation choices.
Unary and row/column operations allow the same complete-source reuse for
small groups on optimized runtime profiles when the source dies at the
operation.  Live small sources retain independent destinations.
Mixed matrix operations use separate independent and tied alternatives for
one-sided source reuse.  Small groups select reuse only for a dying complete
compatible input.  Independent destinations retain early-clobber; a tied
destination reads both source groups before the core writes its result.
Groups occupying at least half the M bank retain the combined register and
matching constraint, avoiding an impossible independent alternative while
letting the allocator coalesce the input and output.
Uniform-type binary operations allow complete source/output overlap for all
runtime M group sizes.  Both inputs are preserved before the operation, and
an overlapping destination needs no separate datatype setup.

Zero-offset integer and floating row/column shifts of basic Squares expand
to complete-mode copies on optimized runtime profiles.  Floating values
retain their bits, including zero signs and NaN payloads.  The move stays
visible to early ownership checking even for an unused result.  Nonzero and
dynamic offsets, row/column broadcasts and O0 retain their existing lowering.

Row/column broadcast indices and shift offsets use SI control operands.
The instructions consume only bits 31:0, independently of XLEN.  Public
size_t/int signatures and any separate full-width uses remain unchanged.
Known zero controls use x0, including constants with nonzero upper bits on
RV64.  The row/column instruction and its datatype preparation remain.

Typed broadcasts use the source scalar carrier mode, bounded by XLEN.
Both descriptors retain XLEN operands.  Argument
evaluation and independent full-width uses keep their original widths.

Typed 8-, 16- and 32-bit data-scalar arithmetic uses the source carrier mode.
Its TC-to-TB conversion and amestype write are unchanged.  Wider carriers,
shift counts and fixed exponents retain Pmode register operands.  Known
zero counts and exponents use x0 without removing the operation, destination
conversion or old-destination input.  Data-scalar all-zero bit patterns also
use x0; amestype, TC-to-TB conversion and the original operation remain.
Nonzero bit patterns, including floating negative zero, keep their carrier.
Narrow operands keep a separate integer carrier when their source is a
floating register view, avoiding cross-class operands during allocation.

Local datatype preparation
--------------------------

Prepared common and unary operations keep proven datatype metadata as constants,
allowing dead GPR definitions to disappear.  Destination setup and scalar
TC writes still require GPR operands; packet and overlap checks are unchanged.

Typed row-major, column-major and strided loads reuse matching Md for the
complete destination group.  Layout, stride and BLK memory extents are unchanged.
The late zero descriptor marker omits only msettyp; the load and its memory
dependency remain.  Unknown or mismatched groups retain datatype setup.
Integer row/column index constructors also reuse complete destination Md;
their index instructions remain.  At optimization levels above zero, the
runtime-N entry bound replaces a call-site representability check when it
already proves that N-1 fits.  Narrower indices retain the local check;
unused volatile constructions still require entry validation.

ACC matmul shares source preparation when both operands are the same
allocated M group, mode and Md.  Output and instruction length use the same
predicate; before allocation, length assumes both sources need preparation.
Private MEM and scratch operands remain in RTL.

Md fact resets clear the occupancy bitmap; lookups reject unoccupied slots
before reading their old contents. GPR constant and identity resets are unchanged.
Branch snapshots copy only occupied Md facts, while retaining all scalar
constants, dynamic identities and descriptor ordering information.

Common input preparation can set Md before a complete group's private raw
reloads instead of saving and reloading that group again at the consumer.
A 64-instruction same-block scan requires every member to be overwritten,
an unchanged descriptor, and compatible input groups. Only private nontrapping
loads, private XLEN scalar saves, plain GPR address calculations and
register-only CPU floating-point operations may intervene, including
recognized min/max, copysign and rounding
patterns. Descriptor dependencies remain checked; floating
operations retain their order and status effects. Calls, state access,
other stores, frame changes and non-call exceptions retain the original preparation.
Identical common inputs with the same packet step and descriptor share one
preparation, using the existing input-order mask. Partial overlaps and
conflicting descriptors keep their original preservation sequences.
Mixed-width consumers may contain several datatype packets in one group.
Each packet gets its own setter before the complete reloads; adjacent
packets are not treated as a single larger datatype group.
Row-major, column-major and strided stores use the same proof for their
single source packet. The user store retains its original memory operands
and position; only its private preservation sequence is replaced.
Conversions and structural unary operations also use this proof for a
complete source group. Destination setup still follows the unary overlap
and datatype rules.
Row/column broadcasts and shifts use the same complete-reload
proof. Their data operation, control operand and destination setup rules
remain unchanged.
Internal complete-value copies use this proof before copying a live old
destination. The source reloads and mmov.m.m remain; destination Md setup
still follows the existing disjoint-group rule.

M-to-ACC transfers apply the complete-reload proof to each source packet,
including tuple and packed groups. Destination Ad setup and the transfers
remain; unproven packets keep their original preservation sequence.
Matmul applies the same proof independently to both complete source groups.
An identical shared input is prepared once. Partial overlap and conflicting
descriptors retain the original path; the old ACC and multiply are unchanged.
Zip/unzip independently proves the complete reloads of its two halves.
Each proven half joins the preparation mask; the tied 1x2 value and the
original permutation remain unchanged.
Same-type integer binary operations use the same reload proof for each
source.  Identical inputs share one setup; partial preparation retains the
existing mask and all arithmetic and user-memory effects.
One immediate or GPR-plus-immediate descriptor definition may move before
the reloads when its input is unchanged and its old result is not observed.
The move and setter insertion occur only after the consumer rewrite succeeds.

msettyp/asettyp clear data, so equal descriptors alone do not justify
removing a clear.  Shared-source reuse is confined to one composite and
retains ACC updates and arithmetic side effects.

ztt_state stops after classification when a function has no typed M/ACC
operands.  Otherwise it selects the call-boundary policy before lowering
and adds physical-bank call clobbers during the same lowering walk.

ztt_md_reuse runs after allocation and scheduling, before variable tracking.
It tracks complete M groups and descriptor values along single-predecessor
fallthrough chains.  Two pending snapshots also reach unique-predecessor
targets of plain scalar conditional branches and direct jumps.  At a
two-predecessor join, the adjacent fallthrough state is intersected with
the cached incoming edge.  Only identical constant Md groups, GPR values
and scalar datatype values survive.  Dynamic descriptor identities and
definition-use metadata are path-local and are discarded.  Missing edges,
larger joins, backedges and exceptional edges discard facts; deeper nesting
can evict a snapshot.  No state preparation is moved across a branch.
Descriptor cleanup after DCE uses the same boundaries; private raw spill
tracking remains confined to each block.
Md queries compare the physical register number and mode directly, without
allocating temporary REG nodes for packet, zip or M/ACC transfer queries.
Narrow broadcast queries read the GPR constant cache directly and truncate
known values to the operand mode without constructing a Pmode REG.
Constant evaluation handles register and integer leaves directly, retaining
XLEN normalization and the general memory/side-effect checks for expressions.
Md invalidation skips unrelated register banks. A conservative flag records
facts that depend directly on a GPR; constants and versioned descriptors do
not require a fact scan for GPR writes. Partial writes retain overlap checks,
and the separate GPR value cache is still invalidated.
A physical-group occupancy bitmap skips empty Md slots during invalidation
and constant-state intersection. Clearing, replacing and copying state keep
the bitmap synchronized; overlap and descriptor identity checks are unchanged.
Cache-block clean, flush, invalidate and zero preserve Md and amestype.
Their memory operations remain in place and stop private spill reuse.
Standard thread-fence patterns preserve register and datatype facts, but
still invalidate private memory reuse.  The fences and user memory accesses
  remain in place.  The operand-free empty asm with only a memory clobber
  also preserves register facts, but invalidates private memory reuse.
  Other inline asm and unknown operations keep their usual kills.
  The same empty-barrier predicate permits removal of dead private
  workspace; all stack-reference and allocation-path checks still apply.
Recognized AME CSR reads preserve Md and scalar-datatype facts while
invalidating the result GPR.  The volatile read and its order are unchanged;
CSR writes, raw operations, acquire/release and asm retain their boundaries.
Post-DCE descriptor cleanup also retains GPR facts across recognized
single-SET ACC clears and M/ACC transfers, invalidating the written result.
Prepared single-SET ACC matmul, value zip/unzip and indexed operations
retain those GPR facts.
Matching matmul and M-to-ACC sources skip save/set/reload; internal operands
control output and instruction length.  M-to-ACC checks Md independently
for each complete transfer packet, including packed ACC groups.  It still
sets every destination Ad and transfers every source packet.  A whole-group
fact cannot substitute for separately prepared packets.
Paired-value zip/unzip checks Md separately for both complete square
groups.  Each matching half skips its save/set/reload; an unmatched half
retains its full preparation and private storage.  When both match, the
prepared form drops the private operands.  The tied value, original
operation and both descriptors remain for subsequent consumers.
Conversion and structural unary operations also reuse complete source
packets.  Destination setup remains unless its complete packets already
match.  The original operation, rounding and status effects remain.  An
overlapping destination must be the same complete source group with the same
descriptor and packet step; partial overlaps do not use the prepared form.

Mixed elementwise, scalar, ternary and indexed operations use the same
per-operand preparation mask after allocation.  Every packet must match,
and an earlier incompatible overlapping setup prevents reuse.  An earlier
identical group with the same descriptor and packet step supplies preparation
for a later operand, including when incoming Md is unknown.  Tied old
destinations participate in this check; scalar TC writes and the original
calculation remain.  Fixed-exponent old-destination operations keep their
unused TC operand as zero rather than allocating a GPR.  Partial preparation
retains the private MEM and scratch.
When every required operand matches, a prepared SET drops the MEM, stride
and scratch dependencies while keeping tied old destinations.  Existing
dead-workspace cleanup then checks whether the dynamic allocation is unused.

After register allocation, pure AME dynamic sizes expand directly from the
saved scale register, without materializing an additional zero term.  Early
expansion, fixed-offset and mixed RVV/AME sizes retain their existing form.

Calls, basic asm, unknown typed operations and unknown UNSPECs clear all facts.
Recognized scalar copysign, min/max, classification, Zfa quiet comparisons
and CPU fcsr/fflags accesses preserve Md and amestype facts. Recognized sNaN
flag helpers and scalar rounding/conversion patterns also preserve these
facts. Rounding accepts only known tags and scalar CPU registers, including
sign-extended integer results. Their instructions and status effects remain; any
result GPR is invalidated. Private spill rules are unchanged.
Recognized CPU atomic loads, stores and read-modify-write patterns preserve
Md and amestype as well. Their GPR results and scratch clobbers invalidate
descriptor facts; memory effects still stop private spill tracking. Atomic
instructions, fences and memory orders remain unchanged.
Scalar clmul, clmulh and clmulr preserve Md and amestype while invalidating
their result GPR facts. Their calculations remain unchanged, including
when emitted by CRC expansion; live spill forwarding still stops.
Scalar brev8, zip/unzip and xperm4/8 also preserve Md and amestype;
their result GPR facts are invalidated. Private spill rules are unchanged.
Recognized scalar SHA256, SHA512, SM3, AES and SM4 operations also preserve
these facts. Cipher controls retain their immediate bounds. Only the result
GPR changes; unknown UNSPECs and live spill forwarding keep their boundaries.
Recognized standard RVV integer, floating, configuration and memory
instructions also preserve Md and amestype. These patterns use GPRs, vector
registers and VL/VTYPE/VXRM; floating classes may also use FPRs and FRM.
Result GPRs still invalidate descriptor facts. RVV memory accesses still
stop private spill tracking. Vector instructions, VL, masks, rounding and
flags remain unchanged. Vendor and unclassified vector instructions retain
the existing boundary behavior.
Empty extended asm with only GPR operands and an optional compiler memory
clobber preserves Md and scalar-datatype facts.  Other extended asm clears
AME state facts but preserves unchanged ordinary GPR constants and value
identities.  Outputs, partial writes and clobbers
invalidate overlapping GPR facts; fixed/global registers are excluded.
Asm goto remains a control-flow boundary.  Private spill tracking stops at
every asm; the workspace predicate still accepts only operand-free barriers.
Register definitions invalidate dependent descriptors and overlapping groups.
Full Pmode GPR single SETs establish constants through RTL simplification.
Prepared groups retain constant snapshots after the original GPR changes;
unknown descriptors follow full-width ordinary GPR copies using local value
identities.  Overwriting one copy does not invalidate a surviving copy.
Direct register writes visit only overlapping GPR cache slots, using the
RTL register span.  Partial and compound writes retain the general overlap
matcher; complete M/ACC writes do not scan unrelated GPR slots.
Partial/parallel definitions invalidate affected register identities and
constants; memory contents are not tracked.
Typed loads and subsequent state operations can use an earlier-defined
ordinary GPR holding the same full descriptor already consumed by a typed
load.  Fixed/global registers and instruction clobbers are excluded.  Multiple
descriptor substitutions in one consumer are validated together, including
full scalar TC values; control operands are unchanged.  Dead materialization
and register notes are updated after substitution.
A use bitmap limits lookup to witnessed descriptors in register order.
Redefinitions, invalidation and joins clear the corresponding witnesses.
ACC-to-M descriptors keep multi-insn constants intact through allocation
when the ACC input is a PHI or comes from the sole immediate predecessor.
Local transfers retain ordinary constant construction to share high parts.
Typed stores with a retained descriptor also establish a constant-use
witness after their explicit clobbers are invalidated.  Following stores
and calculations can reuse the surviving GPR without repeating its setup.
After dead workspace calculations are removed, one further descriptor scan
handles prepared common, unary and same-type binary SETs.  It can recover
constants hidden by removed GPR definitions; calls, basic asm, other parallel and unknown
operations stop reuse.  It does not repeat state preparation or scalar TC
elimination.
Retained TC writes in prepared scalar SETs also establish materialization
witnesses in this scan.  Eliminated TC writes do not establish a witness.
All required TC writes and their values are retained.
Prepared row/column SETs also preserve descriptor GPR facts.  Retained
setters keep their values; control operands and matrix operations remain.
Prepared typed store SETs preserve those facts as well; stores and their
layout operands remain unchanged and still end private spill tracking.
Retained store preparation likewise preserves GPR facts outside its
explicit clobbers and records the descriptor use in this scan.  Its
save, datatype setup, reload and final store remain unchanged.
Complete M constructor SETs also preserve descriptor GPR facts.  Their
datatype setters, scalar inputs and construction instructions remain.
Recognized singleton raw loads/stores preserve descriptor GPR facts in
this scan, as in the main Md scan.
Ordinary ACC register moves and spill transfers preserve Md and descriptor
facts outside their explicit M/GPR clobbers in both scans. The transfer,
actual Ad handling and full payload remain unchanged. Borrowed-bank moves
retain the state boundary because their internal M writes are not clobbers.
After allocation, ordinary ACC register copies drop their unused scale
operand. Memory transfers and borrowed-bank moves retain the dependency.
The scan also removes a singleton raw reload of the unchanged register
from its most recent private spill.  Both accesses must be nonvolatile,
nontrapping spill-slot MEMs with equal addresses and known full sizes
and offsets.  Different address GPRs may match through at most sixteen
local pointer-width definitions, with address expressions capped at
sixteen nodes.  Only unrelated ordinary GPR SETs may intervene.  The store
address proof also accepts pointer-width products, including non-power-of-two
private workspace strides; both input values must match through the same
bounded walk.  At the same program point, matching addends are cancelled
before further expansion to retain the existing node bound.  Other modes,
memory operands and division remain excluded.
Failed structural proofs are retried once with address-context
simplification to compact repeated strides under the same limits.
Successful structural matches retain their original representation.
The store remains; frame operations, fixed/global register definitions, memory or
matrix operations and unknown patterns end this spill fact.  After removing
a reload, a bounded same-block return-tail scan can also remove its private
store.  It requires the canonical spill alias set and no possible reads,
including debug locations, before a normal return.  Known memory fences,
operand-free empty memory barriers and AME CSR reads do not read private
slots; they remain in place while the dead-store scan continues.  Calls,
other asm, unknown operations and exceptional/nonlocal paths retain the store.
Known scalar FP operations without memory operands also allow this scan
to continue, as do plain GPR/FPR USE markers.  Their results, liveness
markers and floating-point status effects remain.
Standard RVV operations also allow this scan to continue when none of their
memory operands can read the private slot.  Vector memory effects, VL and
rounding/status operations remain; unknown or volatile memory stops the scan.
A fallback searches up to 64 local instructions for a matching private
save across interleaved raw members.  Intervening stores must have provably
disjoint spill-slot ranges; reloads must not overwrite the target register.
Address equality is proved separately through bounded pointer expressions.
Known register-only standard RVV instructions may intervene; their GPR
outputs must leave both evolving address expressions unchanged.  Vector
memory instructions still stop this fallback, regardless of alias metadata.
Canonical nontrapping private XLEN scalar reloads may also intervene if
they leave both address expressions unchanged.  User loads and stores
remain boundaries; scalar reloads are neither removed nor substituted.
Calls, asm, state setters, unknown memory, frame changes and control-flow
boundaries stop the search.  This fallback retains the private stores.
After reload cleanup, the last 32 instructions of a return block are
checked for unread private stores.  Known disjoint spill-slot ranges do
not keep another member alive; unknown ranges and overlapping reads do.
The existing alias, debug and exceptional-path checks still apply.
An initial reverse scan shares a no-private-read proof across at most
128 instructions, removing stores without rescanning their suffixes.
Any possible private read ends that fast path; the 32-instruction
per-slot check still handles disjoint reads. Calls, asm, unknown effects,
exceptional paths and frame-related stores retain their existing guards.
Known register-only clmul and scalar crypto instructions do not read private
slots and may appear in this return-tail scan. Their operations remain.
Recognized brev8, CPU zip/unzip and xperm4/8 also permit this scan.
Recognized prepared M computations without memory operands also permit
the scan to continue. Their calculations and status effects remain;
unprepared preservation sequences still stop the scan.
Register-only zero, scalar broadcast and row/column index construction
also permit this scan; their constructors and state effects remain.
Register-only ACC clear/zero, prepared M-to-ACC and matmul, and ACC-to-M
also permit the return-tail scan.  Their calculations, transfers and
Ad/Md effects remain; forms with private memory operands still stop it.
Normal ACC register copies may also intervene, including their M/GPR
scratch clobbers.  Memory copies and borrowed-bank forms remain excluded.
The shared unread-tail proof also removes XLEN GPR stores to private
spill slots.  The same alias, range, nontrapping and frame checks apply.
Dead fixed-size locals do not prevent removal of an unused scalable
workspace.  The matched scalable adjustment pair is removed only after
the existing reference checks; fixed adjustments and CFA remain intact.
Repeated pure Pmode definitions of a surviving descriptor are removed
only when the full value is unchanged.  Fixed/global registers, partial
definitions and frame-related instructions retain their definitions.
Typed integer and canonical FP16/BF16/FP32/FP64 zero construction reuses
the bit clear performed by msettyp for a known descriptor and a complete
group.  Other descriptors and raw zeros retain their instructions.
Setters and arithmetic effects remain.
Typed zero broadcasts between known standard integer descriptors also reuse
the complete-group setter.  The whole scalar carrier must be zero; its dead
setup is removed and register notes are refreshed.  Argument effects remain.
Canonical floating broadcasts use the same rule when source and destination
formats match; their rounding modes may differ.  Cross-format broadcasts,
negative zero and unknown carriers retain their instructions.
Integer and canonical floating ACC zero construction reuses asettyp for
every member; the complete ACC mode and Ad writes remain.
An immediate same-type clear/zero ACC-to-M transfer can construct M zero
packets directly.  The local constructor proves the payload and actual Ad;
it remains an ownership witness.  Calls, asm and memory accesses stop the
proof, and arbitrary ACC values retain their transfer and mismatch checks.
The same proof lets M-to-ACC use the existing complete ACC clear, setting
every Ad.  The M constructor stays visible to early ownership checking;
ordinary DCE can remove its dead payload when no other use remains.
The proof can follow two complete, same-descriptor M/ACC transfers or
exact-type M copies back to the constructor, within the same local window.
Copies share the transfer depth limit.  Intermediate calls remain
ownership witnesses; unknown origins and longer chains retain their moves.
It is disabled at O0.  Prepared ACC moves and matmul with both
sources prepared use patterns without private MEM, stride or scratch
operands.  Prepared matmul retains the tied old ACC and arithmetic effects,
but no longer needs source descriptor GPRs.  Fast RTL DCE removes their dead
address calculations before debug locations are computed.  Frame layout
and runtime-N validation remain unchanged.  Partly prepared matmul keeps
its private operands.
In runtime-profile leaf functions, full-width address arithmetic can use
SP plus an immediate when it equals the tracked FP/scale expression.
Actual stack adjustments establish the relation; conflicting CFG joins
reject it.  Other register values follow the immediately preceding layout
block only when it is the sole predecessor.  Allocation,
memory operations and CFA rules are unchanged.  Ordinary GPR loads/stores
can use the same proven SP-relative address directly, retaining the MEM
mode and attributes; dead intermediate address registers are then removed.
After DCE, leaf functions with no live stack references can
drop an unused AME workspace allocation/release pair.  Both adjustments
use the proven frame-size coefficient.
Scalar parameter loads at fixed nonnegative CFA offsets do not use this
workspace and remain in place, including sign/zero-extended loads and their
debug locations.  Other frame references, writes and escaping addresses
retain the allocation.
Frame-size recognition follows same-block full-width definitions for
bounded additions, shifts and positive constant products.
Cross-block pairs require matching
allocation state at joins and balanced normal exits.  Fixed
frames, N checks and CFA rules remain; calls, asm, real stack storage,
probing, alloca and RVV save areas exclude this cleanup.
When the following descriptor scan removes private spills or reloads,
DCE and the same workspace proof run once more. Descriptor-only changes
do not trigger another workspace scan; the pass does not iterate.
For mixed matmul, a prepared side's unused descriptor is replaced with zero
after allocation.  The mask prevents its use; the other side retains its
descriptor and full save/set/reload sequence.  DCE can then remove the dead
descriptor computation without adding another family of RTL patterns.
Typed row-major, column-major and strided stores establish the source
group's Md for subsequent consumers.  When the complete source group is
already prepared, the store drops its own save/set/reload and private
operands.  The prepared pattern retains the destination MEM, user stride,
store kind and volatile state operation; the store itself stays in place.
ACC-to-M moves establish Md separately for each transfer packet.  Track
those subgroups independently, including packed ACC packets, rather than
treating a multiple-packet destination as one larger prepared group.
When a destination packet already has the required Md, omit its setter
but retain every full ACC-to-M transfer.  Unknown or mismatching
packets retain the complete setup; actual Ad and packed transfers are
unchanged.
Grouped M-to-ACC moves also establish source Md per transfer packet.
Discard old source metadata and explicit scratch clobbers before recording
these subgroups, retaining facts for disjoint M registers.
Same-datatype binary operations establish Md for both complete source
groups and the result.  Record all three after explicit clobbers so later
stores and ACC consumers can reuse their preparation.
Binary operations also reuse matching incoming source Md independently.
Matrix and scalar elementwise operations establish each M packet's Md
separately, with destination facts taking precedence on register reuse.
Scalar operands and amestype are not M datatype state.
Old-destination matrix operations establish Md in destination/source order.
Scalar old-destination operations record only D and the M source, not TC
or the fixed exponent. Compatible overlapping groups keep the last setup.
Whole-register loads and stores retain known physical Md without inferring
datatype from the transferred payload.
When both sources match, a prepared form drops the private memory and
address operands while retaining any required destination setup.
If the independent destination group also has matching Md, the late form
omits its setter and descriptor dependency.  Unknown or mismatched Md
still requires destination setup.
Identical physical binary inputs share their first complete preparation,
including when incoming Md is unknown.
Typed broadcast and index constructors establish their complete result
group's Md for later consumers in the same block.
Broadcasts omit their destination setter when that complete group already
has matching Md.  The scalar conversion, explicit source datatype and
broadcast remain.  Zero constructors still retain the setter that clears
their payload.
Conversions and structural unary operations establish source and result Md
per datatype packet.  Do not combine adjacent packets into a larger fact.
Prepared conversions and structural unary operations omit destination setters
when every source and destination packet already has the requested Md.  The
complete result writes, rounding and status effects remain.
Prepared matrix/scalar elementwise operations and gathers omit known destination
setters after all input packets match.  Destination reuse and scalar TC reuse
are independent; old-destination operations keep their preservation rules.
Row/column broadcasts and shifts establish the same Md for the complete
source and result basic Squares, including an in-place result.
When the complete source Md already matches, a prepared form drops the
private source-preservation envelope.  A matching complete destination Md
also removes its setter and descriptor dependency.  The row/column operation
and control argument remain, with the same state invalidation boundaries
as other consumers.

Leaf matrix ternaries, scalar ternaries and scatters can copy a
live old destination with mmov.m.m after establishing matching complete Md.
Each operand must be a complete non-packed basic Square; the copy keeps
the old destination's mode and datatype even when input types differ.  A backward
DF liveness scan admits only sites whose live M groups fit the profile;
live ACC values and fixed M registers prevent this choice.  The separate
copy has an independent destination and can reuse known source Md.  Calls,
bank pressure and ordinary untyped moves retain their existing paths.
Data Scalar forms also require no live M values beyond their inputs and result
to limit descriptor pressure on GPRs, counting each input's actual group
width.  Signed-long exponents need no amestype descriptor and use the
complete bank-capacity bound without that extra restriction.  They use
the copied old value as its tied destination when available; otherwise
identical wide inputs retain their shared reload path.
The old value and dying matrix inputs count toward simultaneous bank
capacity as well, with equal inputs counted once.
